Article 8 To file a bankruptcy application with the People's Court, a bankruptcy petition and relevant evidence shall be submitted.
The bankruptcy petition shall specify the following:
(1) the basic information of the applicant and the respondent;
(2) the purpose of the application;
(3) the facts and grounds for the application;
(4) other matters that the People's Court deems shall be specified.
Where the debtor files the application, it shall also submit to the People's Court a statement of its property status, a schedule of debts, a schedule of claims, relevant financial and accounting reports, a plan for employee placement, and the status of payment of employee wages and social insurance contributions.

Article 14 The People's Court shall, within twenty-five days from the date of the ruling accepting the bankruptcy application, notify known creditors and make a public announcement.
The notice and announcement shall specify the following:
(1) the name or title of the applicant and the respondent;
(2) the date on which the People's Court accepted the bankruptcy application;
(3) the time limit, place, and matters needing attention for the declaration of claims;
(4) the name or title of the administrator and its address for handling affairs;
(5) the requirement that the debtor's debtors or property holders shall discharge debts or deliver property to the administrator;
(6) the time and place of the first creditors' meeting;
(7) other matters that the People's Court deems shall be notified and announced.

Article 15 From the date of service of the ruling accepting the bankruptcy application on the debtor until the conclusion of the bankruptcy proceedings, the relevant persons of the debtor shall assume the following obligations:
(1) properly keep the property, seals, account books, documents, and other materials in their possession or under their management;
(2) work in accordance with the requirements of the People's Court and the administrator, and truthfully answer inquiries;
(3) attend the creditors' meeting as non-voting participants and truthfully answer questions from creditors;
(4) not leave their place of domicile without permission from the People's Court;
(5) not assume the positions of director, supervisor, or senior officer of any other enterprise.
The term 'relevant persons' in the preceding paragraph refers to the legal representative of the enterprise; upon decision of the People's Court, such persons may include the financial management personnel and other operational management personnel of the enterprise.

Article 25 The administrator shall perform the following duties:
(1) Taking over the debtor's property, seals, account books, documents and other materials;
(2) Investigating the debtor's financial status and preparing a report on such status;
(3) Deciding on the debtor's internal management matters;
(4) Deciding on the debtor's daily expenses and other necessary expenses;
(5) Deciding, before the first creditors' meeting is convened, whether to continue or discontinue the debtor's business;
(6) Managing and disposing of the debtor's property;
(7) Representing the debtor in litigation, arbitration or other legal proceedings;
(8) Proposing to convene a creditors' meeting; and
(9) Other duties that the People's Court considers the administrator shall perform.
Where there are other provisions in this Law governing the duties of the administrator, such provisions shall apply.

Article 31 The administrator shall have the right to request the People's Court to rescind any of the following acts involving the debtor's property committed within one year prior to the acceptance of the bankruptcy application by the People's Court:
(1) Transferring property without compensation;
(2) Trading at an obviously unreasonable price;
(3) Providing property security for debts that are not secured by property;
(4) Paying off debts not yet due in advance; or
(5) Waiving claims.

Article 42 The following debts incurred after the People's Court accepts a bankruptcy application shall constitute community debts:
(1) Debts arising from the request by the administrator or the debtor for the other party to perform a contract that has not been performed by either party;
(2) Debts arising from the management of the debtor's property without cause (negotiorum gestio);
(3) Debts arising from the unjust enrichment of the debtor;
(4) Labor remuneration and social insurance premiums payable for the continued operation of the debtor and other debts arising therefrom;
(5) Debts arising from damage caused by the administrator or relevant persons in the performance of their duties; and
(6) Debts arising from damage caused by the debtor's property.

Article 61 The creditors' meeting shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) to verify claims;
(2) to apply to the People's Court for replacement of the administrator, and to examine the administrator's expenses and remuneration;
(3) to supervise the administrator;
(4) to select and replace members of the creditors' committee;
(5) to decide on the continuation or cessation of the debtor's business;
(6) to adopt a reorganization plan;
(7) to adopt a composition agreement;
(8) to adopt a plan for the administration of the debtor's property;
(9) to adopt a plan for the realization of the bankruptcy property;
(10) to adopt a plan for the distribution of the bankruptcy property;
(11) other functions and powers that the People's Court deems shall be exercised by the creditors' meeting.
The creditors' meeting shall make minutes of the resolutions on the matters discussed.

Article 68 The creditors' committee shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) supervising the management and disposal of the debtor's property;
(2) supervising the distribution of the bankruptcy property;
(3) proposing the convening of a creditors' meeting;
(4) other functions and powers entrusted by the creditors' meeting.
When performing its duties, the creditors' committee shall have the right to require the administrator and relevant persons of the debtor to make explanations or provide relevant documents concerning matters within their functions and powers.
Where the administrator or relevant persons of the debtor refuse to accept supervision in violation of the provisions of this Law, the creditors' committee shall have the right to request the People's Court to make a decision on the matters under supervision; the People's Court shall make a decision within five days.

Article 69 Where the administrator performs any of the following acts, he shall promptly report to the creditors' committee:
(1) transfer of rights and interests in immovable property such as land and houses;
(2) transfer of property rights such as prospecting rights, mining rights, and intellectual property rights;
(3) transfer of all inventory or business;
(4) borrowing;
(5) creation of property security;
(6) transfer of claims and securities;
(7) performance of contracts that have not been fully performed by both the debtor and the counterparty;
(8) waiver of rights;
(9) retrieval of security;
(10) other acts of property disposal that have a material impact on the interests of creditors.
Where no creditors' committee is established, the administrator shall promptly report to the People's Court when performing the acts referred to in the preceding paragraph.

Article 78 During the restructuring period, under any of the following circumstances, upon request of the administrator or any interested party, the People's Court shall make a ruling to terminate the restructuring proceedings and declare the debtor bankrupt:
(1) The debtor's business and property conditions continue to deteriorate, lacking the possibility of rescue;
(2) The debtor has committed fraud, maliciously reduced the debtor's property, or engaged in other acts that are significantly detrimental to the interests of creditors;
(3) Due to the debtor's conduct, the administrator is unable to perform his duties.

Article 81 The draft restructuring plan shall include the following contents:
(1) the debtor's business plan;
(2) classification of claims;
(3) plan for adjustment of claims;
(4) plan for satisfaction of claims;
(5) period for implementation of the restructuring plan;
(6) supervision period for the implementation of the restructuring plan;
(7) other plans that are conducive to the restructuring of the debtor.

Article 82 Creditors of the following categories of claims shall attend the creditors' meeting to discuss the draft restructuring plan and vote on the draft restructuring plan in groups according to the following classification of claims:
(1) claims secured by specific property of the debtor;
(2) wages, medical expenses, disability subsidies, and bereavement pensions owed by the debtor to its employees, basic pension insurance premiums and basic medical insurance premiums that should be credited to the employees' personal accounts, and compensation payments that should be paid to employees according to laws and administrative regulations;
(3) taxes owed by the debtor;
(4) ordinary claims.
When necessary, the People's Court may decide to set up a small-claims group within the ordinary claims group to vote on the draft restructuring plan.

Article 87 If some voting groups fail to adopt the draft reorganization plan, the debtor or the administrator may negotiate with the voting groups that have not adopted the draft reorganization plan. The said voting groups may vote again after the negotiation. The result of the negotiation between the two sides shall not prejudice the interests of other voting groups.
If a voting group that fails to adopt the draft reorganization plan refuses to vote again or still fails to adopt the draft reorganization plan after voting again, but the draft reorganization plan meets the following conditions, the debtor or the administrator may apply to the People's Court for approval of the draft reorganization plan:
(1) Under the draft reorganization plan, the claims set forth in item (1) of paragraph 1 of Article 82 of this Law shall be paid in full from the specific property, the losses suffered by them due to deferred payment shall be fairly compensated, and their security rights shall not be materially impaired, or the said voting group has adopted the draft reorganization plan;
(2) Under the draft reorganization plan, the claims set forth in items (2) and (3) of paragraph 1 of Article 82 of this Law shall be paid in full, or the corresponding voting groups have adopted the draft reorganization plan;
(3) Under the draft reorganization plan, the repayment ratio for ordinary claims shall not be lower than the repayment ratio that they would have received under bankruptcy liquidation proceedings at the time the draft reorganization plan is submitted for approval, or the said voting group has adopted the draft reorganization plan;
(4) The adjustment of the rights and interests of the contributors under the draft reorganization plan is fair and equitable, or the contributors' group has adopted the draft reorganization plan;
(5) The draft reorganization plan treats the members of the same voting group fairly, and the order of repayment of claims provided therein does not violate the provisions of Article 113 of this Law;
(6) The business plan of the debtor is feasible.
If, upon examination, the People's Court considers that the draft reorganization plan complies with the provisions of the preceding paragraph, it shall, within thirty days from the date of receipt of the application, rule to approve the plan, terminate the reorganization proceedings, and make a public announcement.

Article 113 After the bankruptcy expenses and debts of common interest have been paid in priority from the bankruptcy property, the claims shall be repaid in the following order:
(1) wages, medical expenses, disability subsidies, and death benefits owed by the bankrupt to its employees, the basic old-age insurance and basic medical insurance premiums that should have been contributed to the personal accounts of employees, and the compensation required by laws and administrative regulations to be paid to employees;
(2) social insurance premiums owed by the bankrupt other than those specified in the preceding item, and taxes owed by the bankrupt;
(3) ordinary bankruptcy claims.
If the bankruptcy property is insufficient to meet the repayment requirements of the same order, distribution shall be made on a pro rata basis.
The wages of the directors, supervisors, and senior officers of the bankrupt enterprise shall be calculated based on the average wage of the employees of the enterprise.

Article 115 The administrator shall promptly formulate a plan for the distribution of bankruptcy property and submit it to the creditors' meeting for discussion.
The distribution plan for bankruptcy property shall specify the following:
(1) the names or titles and addresses of the creditors participating in the distribution of bankruptcy property;
(2) the amounts of claims participating in the distribution of bankruptcy property;
(3) the amount of bankruptcy property available for distribution;
(4) the order, proportions, and amounts of distribution of bankruptcy property;
(5) the methods for implementing the distribution of bankruptcy property.
After the creditors' meeting adopts the distribution plan for bankruptcy property, the administrator shall submit it to the People's Court for approval by ruling.
